Here's how to solve it:

First, using the Pythagorean theorem in $\triangle ABE$, we have:
\begin{align*}
AE^2 &= AB^2 - BE^2 = 5^2 - BE^2 \\
BE^2 &= AE^2 - 5^2 = (6 - 5)^2 = 1^2 = 1 \\
BE &= \sqrt{1} = \boxed{1} \text{ unit }
\end{align*}

Now, using the height $BE$ to get $BD$:
\begin{align*}
BD &= BC - BD = BC - BE = 5 - 1 = \boxed{4} \text{ unit }
\end{align*}

Next, in $\triangle ADE$, applying the Pythagorean theorem again:
\begin{align*}
AD^2 &= AE^2 + DE^2 = 1^2 + DE^2 \\
DE^2 &= AD^2 - 1 = (6 - 4)^2 = 2^2 = 4 \\
DE &= \sqrt{4} = \boxed{2} \text{ unit }
\end{align*}

Finally, compute the area $\triangle DEC$, which is half of the area of the parallelogram $ABDC$:
\begin{align*}
\text{area of } \triangle DEC &= \tfrac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height} \\
&= \tfrac{1}{2} \times DE \times BC = \tfrac{1}{2} \times 2 \times 5 = \boxed{5} \text{ square units }
\end{align*}

Since the area is in square units, no extra unit is needed.

