Let $b=4\ell$, we now need to find the largest value of $\ell$ for which the quantity $4! \cdot \left( \binom4 {4-\ell} \right)$ is divisible by $\binom{20}{4}$.

By trial and error, we can observe that $4! \cdot \left( \binom 4 {4-1} \right) = 4! \cdot 4 = 112$ is a divisor of $\binom {20} 4$. In fact, $4$ additional values up to $4\ell =16$ will give more factors of 112. Now we have four possible values for $n$: 3, 2, 1, and 0.

If $n=0$, that means all the fours were given to different students, and there is no other student for the teacher to guess. So $n>\text{0}$.

When $n=3$, four students exclusively own four sets of papers, and the remaining 16 students share the other 6 papers. In this case, $\ell=4$ and the sum of $k_i$'s is $4\ell = 16$. The number of valid guesses can be written as $(4! \cdot 4) \cdot \left( \binom {16} 1\right) = 112 \cdot 16$. This is indeed a divisor of $\binom {20} 4 = 184,756$. So, we have one possible value for $n$:

\begin{aligned}
    n &= 3 \\
    k_i &\le 4 \quad \forall\ i=1,2,3,\text{ and } \sum_{i=1}^{3}k_i = 16
\end{aligned}

There are multiple ways to form sums of 16 using three 4s, but we will verify only two cases since they give the same result.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $k_1=k_2=k_3=4$
\item $k_1=3, k_2=4, k_3=5$
\end{enumerate}

Both cases work for the same reason: Since the total number of papers is now 20, the product of the number of valid guesses remains the same; nonetheless, the teacher can correctly guess one student less compared to Case 1. As a result, the fraction answering the original problem will be reduced as well:

\boxed{ \frac{112}{112\cdot 16} = \frac 1{144} }

