completions_6:
The volume of a cube is defined by the formula V = s³, where s is the side length of the cube. If we know the volume, we can find the side length, then find the surface area.

First, we'll find s:
\begin{align*}
V &= 0.027 \ \text{ cubic meters} \\
s³ &= 0.027 \ \text{ cubic meters} \\
s^{3} &= \frac{0.027 \ \text{ cubic meters}}{1} \\
s &= \sqrt[3]{\frac{0.027 \ \text{ cubic meters}}{1}} \\
s &= \boxed{\sqrt[3]{0.027} \ \text{ meters}}
\end{align*}

Now, let's find the surface area. The surface area of a cube is defined by the formula A = 6s².

\begin{align*}
A &= 6s² \\
A &= 6 \left( \sqrt[3]{0.027} \ \text{ meters} \right)^2 \\
A &= 6 \cdot \left( \frac{\sqrt[3]{0.027}}{\sqrt{3}} \right)^2 \\
A &= 6 \cdot \left( \frac{\sqrt[3]{0.027}}{\sqrt{3}} \right)\left( \frac{\sqrt[3]{0.027}}{\sqrt{3}} \right) \\
A &= 6 \cdot \frac{1}{3} \cdot \frac{\sqrt[3]{0.027}}{\sqrt[6]{3}} \cdot \frac{\sqrt[3]{0.027}}{\sqrt[6]{3}} \\
A &= \boxed{\frac{2 \cdot \sqrt[3]{0.027}}{3} \ \text{ square meters}}
\end{align*}

To check the units, we'll perform a multiplication:

\begin{align*}
6 \cdot \left(\frac{\text{square meters}}{\text{square meters}}\right) &= 6 \ \text{ square meters} \\
\text{and } \left(\frac{\text{cubic meters}}{1}\right)^{2/3} &= \frac{\text{cubic meters}}{\sqrt[3]{1}} = \frac{\text{cubic meters}}{ 1} = \text{square meters}
\end{align*}

That shows that the units are consistent.

If you want to remove the cube root:

\begin{align*}
A &= \frac{2 \cdot \sqrt[3]{0.027}}{3} \ \text{ square meters} \\
A &= \boxed{\frac{2 \cdot \sqrt[3]{0.027}}{3} \cdot \left(\frac{3}{\sqrt[3]{3}} \right)^2 \ \text{ square meters}} \\
A &= \left( \frac{2}{\sqrt[3]{3}} \right)^2 \cdot \sqrt[3]{0.027} \ \text{ square meters} \\
A &= \boxed{\left( \frac{2}{\sqrt[3]{3}} \right)^2 \cdot \sqrt{5. 551796} \ \text{ square meters}}
\end{align*}

